"use strict";(self.webpackChunkspa_template=self.webpackChunkspa_template||[]).push([[780],{6780:s=>{s.exports=JSON.parse('{"created":"2017-04-23T17:22:00","modified":"2017-04-23T17:22:00","id":1032,"title":"MyClass.Create() vs new MyClass()","article":"<p>A basic programming pattern that is seen more recently<sup>1</sup> has been developers providing &quot;Create&quot; or &quot;New&quot; method rather than a constructor. </p>\\n<p>*<sup>1</sup> A real world example being AesManaged in the Cryptography package. In the full framework it has a constructor, but when the class was transferred into its own nuget package for .Net Standard (and core) they removed the AesManager class and made a new class called Aes, which was almost exactly the same class except it no longer had a public constructor*</p>\\n<h4 id=\\"fig-1-example\\">Fig 1 Example</h4>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">class</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">MyClass</span>\\n{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">protected</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">MyClass</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"></span>)</span>\\n    {\\n        Description = <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"Hello World\\"</span>;\\n    }\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">static</span> MyClass <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Create</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"></span>)</span> =&gt; <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> MyClass();\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">string</span> Description { <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">get</span>; <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">set</span>; }\\n}</code></pre>\\n<h2 id=\\"why\\">Why?</h2>\\n<p>In a nutshell it gives a developer greater flexibility in handling the creation of an object and what is returned. Constructors are just methods, however they have limitations on what they can return. </p>\\n<p>Static methods like this work well with Microsofts recommendation about keeping constructors simple <a href=\\"https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/ms229060(v=vs.110).aspx\\">see Constructor Design</a> and using static factory methods for anything more complex.</p>\\n<p>They can help with a variety of things:</p>\\n<ul>\\n<li><a href=\\"#error-handling\\">Error handling</a></li>\\n<li><a href=\\"#async-creation\\">Async Creation</a></li>\\n<li><a href=\\"#pre-defined-classes\\">Pre-Defined Classes</a></li>\\n<li><a href=\\"#other-advantages\\">Other</a></li>\\n</ul>\\n<p>As with all programming patterns there are also a couple <a href=\\"#drawbacks\\">drawbacks</a> too.</p>\\n<h3 id=\\"error-handling\\">Error Handling</h3>\\n<p>A developer only has 2 ways in which to handle errors in a constructor, return a malformed object or throw an Exception. </p>\\n<p>Malformed objects can be used inadvertently after creation and Exceptions are relatively expensive so should only be used for unexpected errors. More importantly though Exceptions dont give an explicit API surface for when things that have gone wrong, so to learn the error handling I have to read the docs, or go throw code to find the potential exceptions thrown.</p>\\n<p>Using a static method gives us more options, the simplest being we could return null, which would indicate the object couldnt be created but also wouldn&#39;t return a corrupt object to be used by mistake.</p>\\n<p>Sometimes we want to know more information about what went wrong, although who says we need to return an object of MyClass? As we are no longer using constructors we can return anything.</p>\\n<p><a href=\\"#fig-2\\">Fig.2</a> Shows a way of creating an object, but also giving feedback to the calling code as to the success of creating the object, plus information.</p>\\n<h4 id=\\"fig-2---returning-different-types\\">Fig 2 - Returning Different Types</h4>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">class</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Result</span>&lt;T&gt;\\n{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">bool</span> IsSuccess { <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">get</span>; <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">set</span>; }\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> T Data { <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">get</span>; <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">set</span>; }\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">string</span> Error { <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">get</span>; <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">set</span>; }\\n}</code></pre>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// Updated Create method from Fig.1</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">static</span> Result&lt;MyClass&gt; <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Create</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"></span>)</span> \\n{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">if</span>(somethingIsWrong) {\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> Result&lt;MyClass&gt;();\\n    }   \\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> Result&lt;MyClass&gt; {\\n        IsSuccess = <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>,\\n        Data = <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> MyClass();\\n    }\\n}</code></pre>\\n<p><em><strong>NB:</strong> The Result&lt;T&gt; class can be made alot more convenient to use than this, a more complete example can be found <a href=\\"https://github.com/lski/Lski.Fn\\">here</a></em></p>\\n<p>A major advantage about handling errors in this way as it makes Fluent programming much easier. As the error can be passed along to the next method in the chain.</p>\\n<h3 id=\\"async-creation\\">Async Creation</h3>\\n<p>Using a static method, you can also create objects using async/await. These are not available using constructors, so often developers create a two stage process like in <a href=\\"#fig-3\\">Fig.3</a></p>\\n<p><strong>NB:</strong> This example shows logic that would be better placed in a factory method see <a href=\\"#fig-7\\">Fig.7</a> so that it can be tested independently, but is useful for an example.</p>\\n<h4 id=\\"fig-3\\">Fig 3</h4>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">class</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">BlogData</span>\\n{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">private</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">readonly</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">string</span> _url;\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">private</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">bool</span> _isLive;\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">BlogData</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">string</span> url</span>)</span>\\n    {\\n        _url = url;\\n    }\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">async</span> Task <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Init</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"></span>)</span>\\n    {\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">using</span> (<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> client = <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> HttpClient())\\n        {\\n            <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> response = <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">await</span> client.GetAsync(_url);\\n\\n            _isLive = response.IsSuccessStatusCode;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}</code></pre>\\n<p>The problem here is the object is not fully created until the Init() function is called. But if using a static function this could be all incorporated into one function.</p>\\n<p><a href=\\"#fig-4\\">Fig.4</a> ensures the object can only be created in a single step process.</p>\\n<h4 id=\\"fig-4\\">Fig 4</h4>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">class</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">BlogData</span>\\n{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">private</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">readonly</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">string</span> _url;\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">private</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">readonly</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">bool</span> _isLive;\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">private</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">BlogData</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">string</span> url, <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">bool</span> isLive</span>)</span>\\n    {\\n        _url = url;\\n        _isLive = isLive;\\n    }\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">static</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">async</span> Task&lt;BlogData&gt; <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Create</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">string</span> url</span>)</span>\\n    {\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">using</span> (<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> client = <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> HttpClient())\\n        {\\n            <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> response = <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">await</span> client.GetAsync(url);\\n\\n            <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> BlogData(url, response.IsSuccessStatusCode);\\n        }\\n    }\\n}</code></pre>\\n<h3 id=\\"pre-defined-classes\\">Pre-Defined Classes</h3>\\n<p>As static functions can be named anything you want, it can help by giving meaning to constructors, describing the type of object being created. Just using constructors means you are restricted to using overloads with different signatures, which can be hard to decipher.</p>\\n<p><a href=\\"#fig-5\\">Fig.5</a> Shows the Result class mentioned in Error handling, by using named functions we can infer meaning to the classes created. In this case a Failed or Successful result.</p>\\n<p>If just relying on a constructor, it can be hard to know exactly what the intention will be, as the only difference can be the signature of the parameters.</p>\\n<h4 id=\\"fig-5\\">Fig 5</h4>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">class</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Result</span>&lt;T&gt;\\n{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">private</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Result</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"></span>)</span> {}\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">static</span> Result&lt;T&gt; <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Success</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\">T data</span>)</span> =&gt; <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> Result&lt;T&gt;()\\n    {\\n        Data = data,\\n        IsSuccess = <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>\\n    };\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">static</span> Result&lt;T&gt; <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Failed</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">string</span> error</span>)</span> =&gt; <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> Result&lt;T&gt;\\n    {\\n        Error = error,\\n        IsSuccess = <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">false</span>\\n    };\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">bool</span> IsSuccess { <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">get</span>; <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">private</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">set</span>; }\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> T Data { <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">get</span>; <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">private</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">set</span>; }\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">string</span> Error { <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">get</span>; <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">private</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">set</span>; }\\n}</code></pre>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">static</span> MyClass <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Create</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"></span>)</span> \\n{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> somethingIsWrong \\n        ? Result&lt;MyClass&gt;.Success(<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> MyClass()) \\n        : Result&lt;MyClass&gt;.Failed(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"Oops\\"</span>);\\n}</code></pre>\\n<p>A positive side effect of using static functions is that the developer of the class can enforce certain rules that would be difficult to achieve without using static classes. This can result in an object being in a malformed state.</p>\\n<p>As shown in <a href=\\"#fig-6\\">Fig.6</a> you can also get situations where you can get an unintentional corruption of the created object, or would require extensive data checking even in this small example.</p>\\n<h4 id=\\"fig-6\\">Fig 6</h4>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">class</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Result</span>&lt;T&gt;\\n{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Result</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">bool</span> isSuccess, T data, <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">string</span> error</span>)</span> {\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">//...</span>\\n    }\\n}</code></pre>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> result = <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> Result(<span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>, <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">null</span>, <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"Oops\\"</span>);</code></pre>\\n<h3 id=\\"other-advantages\\">Other Advantages</h3>\\n<p>There are a few other advantages too, although these can be seen as anti-patterns as they directly tie code to implementation and would be better handled in combination with dependency injection.</p>\\n<h4 id=\\"factory-functions\\">Factory Functions</h4>\\n<p>You can use static <code>Create</code> functions as factory functions, returning different subclass types depending on criteria supplied. </p>\\n<p>However this normally requires specific logic, so should be avoided as it can be considered an anti-pattern because it would make testing that logic harder. It would be better as an instance method on a factory class as seen in <a href=\\"#fig-7\\">Fig.7</a> that can be used with dependency injection and can be swapped out for mocking that logic in tests. If using a static method in code directly it would be impossible to switch out.</p>\\n<h4 id=\\"fig-7\\">Fig 7</h4>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">class</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Factory</span> : <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">IFactory</span>\\n{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> MyClass <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Create</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"></span>)</span>\\n    {\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> MyClass(); <span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// or MyClass.Create();</span>\\n    }\\n}</code></pre>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> factory = <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> Factory();\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> obj = factory.Create();</code></pre>\\n<p>Another advantage would be to create singletons, without the need for A <code>MyClass.Default</code> property where each time the <code>Create</code> function is called it returns the singleton as show in <a href=\\"#fig-8\\">Fig.8</a>. However again this can be considered an anti-pattern and should be controlled by a dependency injection system if one is available as that it can dispose of singletons correctly if needed.</p>\\n<h4 id=\\"fig-8\\">Fig 8</h4>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">class</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">MyClass</span>\\n{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">private</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">static</span> MyClass _instance;\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">protected</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">MyClass</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"></span>)</span>\\n    {\\n        Description = <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"Hello World\\"</span>;\\n    }\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">static</span> MyClass <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Create</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"></span>)</span>\\n    {\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> _instance ?? (_instance = <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> MyClass());\\n    }\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">string</span> Description { <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">get</span>; <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">set</span>; }\\n}</code></pre>\\n<h2 id=\\"drawbacks\\">Drawbacks?</h2>\\n<p>As with any programming pattern there are always couple of drawbacks to be aware, both are related to the fact your constructor is now private/internal.</p>\\n<h3 id=\\"generics-new\\">Generics &quot;new()&quot;</h3>\\n<p>If using a private constructor you wont be able to use the class with a generics where there is a &quot;new()&quot; constraint as that constraint requires a type to have a public parameterless constructor.</p>\\n<h3 id=\\"serializers-and-dynamic-class-generation\\">Serializers and Dynamic class Generation</h3>\\n<p>Most serializers such as Json.net handle serializing classes with a private parameterless constructor perfectly fine, however there is the chance you one you want to use doesnt.</p>\\n<p>If creating classes dynamically in your own code you just need to be aware to use the overload for Activator.CreateInstance that allows nonPublic constructors.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> obj = Activator.CreateInstance(<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">typeof</span>(MyClass), <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>);</code></pre>\\n<h2 id=\\"conclusion\\">Conclusion</h2>\\n<p>Using static methods as constructors really gives us alot of flexibility and benefits, I have started using them more often in code, however they are not for every situation. </p>\\n<p>Also when using them, use them along with benefits of dependency injection, not instead of it.</p>\\n","showInResults":true,"tags":["c#","classes"]}')}}]);